From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nivogli <224451320+nivogli@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 17:00:02 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Size tessellation scratch buffers from reflected shader
 output sizes

Tessellated draws allocate vertex, control-point and patch output buffers
using the device maximum component counts, for an indirect draw 1,048,576
vertices times 496 bytes. Read the sizes the shaders really write from
Metal pipeline reflection, so most of that memory is not allocated at all.
An unknown or implausible size keeps the previous default.

An indirect draw larger than the vertex budget used to write past these
buffers. The conversion kernels now clamp such a draw to the budget.

Adopted from https://github.com/nivogli/MoltenVK commits
a6289be3e28ddbf7f9d87bd09f65946edebc6df7 (sizing, same idea as
https://github.com/KhronosGroup/MoltenVK/pull/2892) and the clamp of
bb88c75c7b28a235d1a7c519b0fda3acf60bb370.

Modifications: statistics, the completion handler and the log output of
those commits are dropped. MVK_PERF_TESS_INDIRECT_VERTS is removed: the
triangle-fan index buffers use the same constant, so a budget above
kMVKMaxDrawIndirectVertexCount could overflow them. The clamp now takes
that constant. Buffer lengths are computed in NSUInteger, so a large
reflected stride cannot wrap in 32 bits.

The tess control output stride is not compared with the tess evaluation
input size, as the vertex output stride is with the tess control input:
the render pipeline compiler requests no reflection. They should match,
since the evaluation stage is converted from the control stage's outputs,
but that is unverified.

MVK_PERF_TESS_REFLECTED_SIZES=0 restores the previous buffer sizes. The
clamp has no switch: it acts only where the base writes out of bounds.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
